course: dictionary
lesson: 10821
title: Vehicle
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A vehicle is something that carries people or things from place to place. A car, bus, truck, train or bike is a vehicle. Vehicle is a noun, and its plural is vehicles.
explain: «Smoke from vehicles, factories and burning rubbish pollutes the air.» Here vehicles means many things that carry people or things, so you can count them. Find the word in the sentence, then ask: what kind of thing does it name?
explain: Vehicle has a second meaning. A story can be a vehicle for a lesson, so the story carries the lesson to you, just as a bus carries you to school. The word is always about carrying something, whether people, goods or an idea.
fact: A vehicle carries people or things from place to place.
fact: Vehicle is a noun, and its plural is vehicles.
fact: In maths, you can count vehicles and their parts: 3 vehicles with 4 wheels each have 12 wheels in all.
fact: «Emergency vehicles» have sirens, so look out for them and wait well back.
fact: «Electric vehicles» run on batteries.
exercise: «Smoke from vehicles, factories and burning rubbish pollutes the air.» What is a vehicle? | *Something that carries people or things from place to place | A type of smoke | A piece of burning rubbish | A kind of factory
exercise: There are 3 vehicles, and each has 4 wheels. How many wheels in all? | *12 | 7 | 1 | 34
exercise: What do electric vehicles run on? | *Batteries | Wind | Coal | Petrol
test: Which of these is a vehicle? | A tree | A cloud | *A truck | A bus
test: «A tram runs on rails and cannot swerve.» What is a tram in this sentence? | A rule for drivers | A kind of rail | *A vehicle that carries people | A bike lane
exam: «Getting on and off buses and trains is a time when children are close to big vehicles whose drivers cannot see everything.» Why should you wait well back? | Because buses are always empty | *Because the driver may not see you | Because vehicles are slow | Because trains never stop
